<description>
Connect hollow rectangular frames into one shortest visible corridor network.

Input structure
- A single foreground color forms disconnected hollow rectangular frames on the background.
- A visible candidate gap is horizontal or vertical, has overlapping frame interiors, and is clear background between the two frames.

Rule
- Select the minimum-length spanning set of visible frame-to-frame gaps.
- For a horizontal selected gap, paint the two boundary rows of the overlapping interiors across the gap, then erase the facing wall cells strictly between those rows.
- For a vertical selected gap, do the transposed operation with boundary columns and the facing top/bottom wall cells.

Key insight
- The connector is determined by the overlap of the rooms' interiors, and longer redundant visible gaps are ignored once the shortest spanning network connects all frames.
</description>
<annotation>
{
  "type": "move_to_match",
  "src": "pair of hollow rectangular frames s",
  "dst": "clear horizontal or vertical gap d between the frames",
  "match_cond": "s.interiors overlap across d and d contains only background cells",
  "transform": "paint d on the two boundary rows or columns of the interior overlap; erase facing wall cells strictly inside the connector opening",
  "multi_step": "choose visible gaps by increasing gap length as a spanning tree until all frames are one 4-connected object"
}
</annotation>
<generation_spec>
```yaml
spec:
  grid: 30x30 fixed
  background: 0
  foreground: single color (always 3 [observed])
  rooms:
    count: 6-8 non-overlapping hollow rectangular frames
    dimensions: 3-11 rows x 3-11 cols; mix of small (3-4) and medium/large (5-12)
    structure: exactly 1-cell-thick border, interior is background
  candidate_edges:
    definition: |
      pair of rooms separated horizontally or vertically with:
      (a) overlapping interiors in the perpendicular axis (>=1 cell),
      (b) the rectangular gap region between them is all background
    count: n_rooms-1 to n_rooms (nearly a tree; at most 1 extra edge)
    gap_distance: 1-7 cells between facing walls
    overlap: 1-7 cells of shared interior range
  spanning_tree:
    algorithm: Kruskal MST by gap distance; all same-gap edges connecting new components added together
    chosen_edges: exactly n_rooms - 1 (forms a spanning tree)
  output_transform:
    for each chosen edge, draw a corridor connecting two rooms:
      horizontal - paint top/bottom rows of overlap across gap; erase facing wall cells between those rows
      vertical - paint left/right cols of overlap across gap; erase facing wall cells between those cols
    result is one 4-connected component

generation_strategy:
  approach: output-first (build connected corridor network, then remove corridors to get input)
  steps:
    1. Choose n_rooms in [6,8]. Build a random spanning tree on n_rooms nodes.
    2. For each tree edge, assign direction (h or v) and gap distance (1-7).
    3. Place rooms on 30x30 grid using tree structure. For each room pick size 3-11 x 3-11.
       Place rooms so each tree edge has the assigned gap and >=1 interior overlap.
       Use constraint propagation or sequential placement along BFS order from root.
    4. Verify all rooms fit in grid, don't overlap, and gap regions are clear background.
       Optionally add 0-1 extra candidate edges (redundant paths rejected by MST).
    5. Draw all hollow frames. This is the INPUT.
    6. Run solver (_spanning_edges + _apply_edge) to produce OUTPUT.
    Retry placement if constraints fail; tree-guided placement keeps rejection low.
```
</generation_spec>
